            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is fairly air tight, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through the weather stripping along the upstairs windows, and around the attic access hatch above the washer and dryer. I recommend you use standard caulking around the attic hatch to create a seal. 



Air leakage was also observed through the two upstairs bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model, controled by a humistat. As we discussed, a humidistat connected to these two exhaust fans will help control the moisture levels upstairs to help reduce the condensation/frosting issues you have experienced. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 120&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

















&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with R5 rigid insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 510&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;













&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 

The two living room, as well as master bedroom windows I recommend to replace. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 10 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 500&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;16.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



























&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Current Oil Boiler and Domestic Hot Water&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



You currently have a 170 litre electric hot water tank, which is also indirectly heated by the Oil Boiler. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

It would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water year round, instead of turning off the boiler during the summer months and using the electricity to heat your hot water (winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year). This can be done by turning down the thermostats around the home. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your current boiler to heat your hot water year round, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;95% Propane Boiler and Domestic Hot Water&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



If you choose to upgrade your boiler to a condensing 95% AFUE Propane boiler, and continue to heat your hot water indrectly with the new boiler, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save an additional $ 130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 









&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 87% AFUE, and appears to be fairly new. When it comes time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 720&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;20.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Window Condensation/Frosting&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



As your home is fairly air tight, mechanical ventilation is important to ensure good air quality for you and your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, odors, and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. 



An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality, to which you already have one installed. The heat recovery ventilator draws stale air from the main floor bathroom, while introducing fresh air into the living room (and is controlled by the humidistat in the living room). The upstairs evnviroment is mainly controlled by the two bathroom fans, which as previously mentioned in "air sealing" can be controlled by a humidistat as well. New fans can also be much more efficient, saving you money on your electrical bill. Generally, a humidistat is set between 40% and 50% of the relative humidity, however as we discussed this "set point" may vary from home to home, also depending on time of year. If you notice the windows are accumulating moisture, lower the setting on the humidistat. Vice versa, if you notice the air is too dry, raise the setting levels. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fireplace Insert&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;With the Current Oil Boiler&lt;/b&gt;

By using your fireplace for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 570 per year in energy savings at $341 per cord of wood.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



&lt;font color="lightseagreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
